This is an advisory/question — answer only, no file edits. The user wants prioritization guidance: in a MLOPS course, what matters most for grading.

Key insight: It's an MLOPS course, NOT an ML research course. The model quality (accuracy) matters less than the engineering around it. Graders care about:

1. **The ML model itself is a means, not the point** — OULAD is well-studied; getting AUC 0.8 is table stakes. Nobody gives extra points for +0.02 AUC. But temporal leakage done RIGHT is an MLOPS point (data management discipline).
2. **End-to-end product** — the requirement doc explicitly says "product hoàn chỉnh, không chỉ dừng ở việc huấn luyện mô hình".
3. The 8 mandatory components map directly to grading rubric: ML model, LLM component, backend/UI, data pipeline + quality checks, separate model/system evaluation, uncertainty handling, reproducibility/versioning/testing, deployment + logging/monitoring.

What to focus on most for an MLOPS course:

**Priority 1 — Pipeline & reproducibility (the "L" in MLOPS):** data pipeline with as-of/temporal integrity, versioning (data/code/model), automated tests, CI. This is the differentiator between "ML project" and "MLOPS project". If you only deliver a notebook + model, you fail the course intent.

**Priority 2 — Working system end-to-end (M3):** backend + UI + LLM + model integrated, deployable via container, monitoring/logging. The demo needs to work.

**Priority 3 — Evaluation discipline:** separate model quality vs system quality, plus the responsibility items the topic itself calls out: temporal leakage, explainability, fairness, responsible prediction. Note the summary.txt explicitly flags: "Chú ý: temporal leakage, explainability, fairness và sử dụng prediction có trách nhiệm" — these 4 things are named in the assignment topic, so they're almost certainly graded.

**Priority 4 — LLM integration quality:** groundedness, fallback, uncertainty handling — shows system thinking.

De-prioritize:
- Squeezing +AUC (hyperparameter tuning beyond reasonable)
- Fancy UI (functional > beautiful)
- Real-time/streaming (explicitly out of scope)
- Massive scale (1 machine is fine)

Also mention: the 4 named points in the topic (temporal leakage, explainability, fairness, responsible use) are the "differentiators" — they're what the course wants you to demonstrate understanding of. A model card, fairness audit, temporal audit are cheap to do but high grading value.

Also practical: demo day risk — end-to-end working demo early (M3 week 7), not week 10. Integration is where teams die.

Map to our task doc: M2 (model v1 with audits) and M3 (E2E) are the critical path; week 8 hardening is the MLOPS core.

Suggest time allocation roughly: 30% data/model, 40% system/pipeline/CI/deploy, 20% evaluation+audits, 10% docs/demo? Something like that.

Keep answer concise, structured, Vietnamese. No tools needed — pure advisory. Maybe end with an offer: incorporate this priority into the task doc (note at section 7 already says "không cắt evaluation"). Could offer to adjust task doc weights but not push.
